<?xml version="1.0"?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="depot_marker">
    <static>true</static>
    <link name="marker_link">
      <visual name="marker_visual">
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>1.0</radius>
            <length>0.1</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.8 0.5 0.0 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1.0 0.7 0.0 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.3 0.2 0.0 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- NO <collision>. THIS IS A PAINTED PAD, NOT A BOLLARD, and the
           difference cost a mission.

           MEASURED 2026-07-31, gz-sim 8, 4 scouts / 3 excavators / 3 haulers on
           lunar_psr.sdf: with a 1.0 m radius collision cylinder on this model,
           scout_04 drove onto the depot marker at the crater centre and jammed
           against it at (-99.9, -149.0) -- 1.005 m from the marker origin,
           against a 1.0 m marker radius and a 0.5847 m robot footprint radius.
           world_odometry_node reported it 208 s into the run:
             scout_04: WHEELS TURNING, BODY NOT MOVING. 9.9 m of wheel travel
             over 20s, 2.58 m covered (74% slip).
           and it never got free.

           The consequence was out of all proportion to a 0.1 m disc:
           htn_planner's SelectSite resolves only when EVERY survey task is
           COMPLETED (htn_planner.py:313-330), so one scout wedged on a marker
           meant no site was ever selected, no excavate or haul task was ever
           generated, and the whole ISRU cycle deadlocked with nine healthy
           robots idle.

           A marker exists to say "the depot is here". Nothing reads it, nothing
           docks with it, and there is no version of this world in which a 10 cm
           painted disc should stop a rover -- least of all now that the depot
           sits in the middle of the ice field every scout surveys and every
           hauler drives to. Visual only.

           Second benefit, smaller but real: scripts/check_terrain.sh drops a
           probe at the depot's XY to measure the collision SURFACE there. With
           a collision cylinder on this model a probe dropped in a world
           containing it would rest on the marker instead of the ground and
           report a surface 0.1 m too high. -->
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
